    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>In the Egyptian account <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> was misled by two nomads sent by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, and his army was attacked while strung out on the march.</li><li>Almost everything known about the battle comes from Egyptian inscriptions and reliefs, whose bombastic tone is recognised; the Hittite side survives only in scattered references.</li><li>Neither empire could defeat the other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and about 15 years later they made a peace treaty that survives in Hittite and Egyptian versions.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A large replica of the Egyptian-Hittite peace treaty of about 1258 BCE hangs at the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, as the earliest international peace treaty known to historians.</p></aside>
    </section>

