    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1550 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>' 15th Dynasty is expelled and the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> begin reclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s borders</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1274 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> leaves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a> with four divisions and marches along the coast through Gaza</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1274 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Two Shasu nomads give <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a> a false report that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> are far off at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1274 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> cross the Orontes, scatter the Re division and attack the Egyptian camp</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">May 1274 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">An Egyptian counterattack, the arrival of the Ne'arin and the Ptah division drive the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> back to the river</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1258 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> make a peace treaty in the 21st year of Ramesses' reign</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-kadesh" data-v="1ea7fd6bed" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The Hittites wait behind Kadesh</b> Muwatalli had placed his troops behind Old Kadesh. Ramesses had been told that the Hittite army was far off, so he was with the Amun division and his bodyguard while the Re division was still coming up to the camp.</li><li><b>Hittite chariots cross the river and scatter the Re division</b> The Hittite chariots crossed the river and charged the middle of the Re division as it marched towards Ramesses. Caught in the open, the division scattered in all directions.</li><li><b>The chariots attack the camp</b> The Hittite chariotry rounded to the north and attacked the Egyptian camp through the Amun shield wall, and panic spread among the Amun division. Some of the Re division fled north to the camp, pursued by Hittite chariots.</li><li><b>Ramesses charges into the Hittite ranks</b> The momentum of the Hittite attack waned as chariots were impeded by obstacles in the camp. Ramesses defeated the first attackers and personally led several charges with his guard, some Amun chariots and survivors of the Re division.</li><li><b>The Egyptian counter-attack drives them to the river</b> The Hittites, believing their enemies routed, stopped to plunder the camp. An Egyptian counter-attack then drove them back towards the Orontes and away from the camp, and in the pursuit lighter Egyptian chariots overtook Hittite chariots.</li><li><b>Muwatalli sends another thousand chariots</b> As the retreat reached the river, Muwatalli, who still held a large reserve of chariots and infantry and the walls of Kadesh, ordered another thousand chariots to counter-attack. High nobles close to the king led them.</li><li><b>The Ne'arin surprise the Hittites at the camp</b> As the Hittite force approached the same Egyptian camp again, the Ne'arin from Amurru arrived without warning and surprised them.</li><li><b>The Ptah division arrives from the south</b> Finally the Ptah division arrived from the south. It came up behind the Hittite force that was pressing on the camp and threatened its rear.</li><li><b>The Hittites are pinned against the Orontes</b> After six unsuccessful charges the Hittite forces were almost surrounded and the survivors were pinned against the Orontes. They abandoned their chariots and tried to swim the river, and many drowned. The Hittites held on to Kadesh.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seti-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seti-i.jpg" alt="Seti I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SetiI-KneelingStatueOfferingToOsiris-CloseUp_MetropolitanMuseum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Keith Schengili-Roberts, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a>' 15th Dynasty around 1550 BCE, the rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom of Egypt</a> grew more aggressive in reclaiming control of their borders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> and Amenhotep II fought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a> north to the Orontes, including conflict with Kadesh. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> tell of declining Egyptian influence, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iv">Thutmose IV</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> lost territory to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, who died in 1292 BCE, campaigned there again and turned Egyptian interest back to the area.</p><p>The Nineteenth Dynasty continued this. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a>, like his father before him, set out to restore the empire of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>. Inscriptions on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> walls record his campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a> and Syria; he made an informal peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, and a second campaign led to his capture of Kadesh and the kingdom of Amurru. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> campaigned with him. At some point both regions may have lapsed back under Hittite control. What happened to Amurru is disputed: the Hittitologist Trevor R. Bryce suggests that it more likely remained a Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state.</p><p>In the fourth year of his reign Ramesses II marched into Syria, either to recapture Amurru or as a probe of his vassals' loyalty and of the terrain for battles. A large weapons order of the year before is recorded. He launched the expedition of his fifth year from his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pi-ramesses">Pi-Ramesses</a> (modern Qantir) in May 1274 BCE, passing the fortress of Tjaru and following the coast to Gaza. The Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> gave the recovery of Amurru as his reason for marching south.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chariot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chariot.jpg" alt="Chariot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chariot">Chariot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A0%D0%B8%D1%81%D1%83%D0%BD%D0%BA%D0%B8_%D0%BA_%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%8C%D0%B5_%C2%AB%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D1%8B%D0%B5_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BD%D0%B8%D1%86%D1%8B%C2%BB._%D0%92%D0%BE%D0%B5%D0%BD%D0%BD%D0%B0%D1%8F_%D1%8D%D0%BD%D1%86%D0%B8%D0%BA%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%8F_%D0%A1%D1%8B%D1%82%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_(%D0%A1%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%BA%D1%82-%D0%9F%D0%B5%D1%82%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B1%D1%83%D1%80%D0%B3,_1911-1915).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sytin Military Encyclopedia, 1912, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a> led an army of four divisions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Re, Set and the apparently newly formed Ptah division. A poorly documented troop called the Ne'arin, possibly Canaanite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, had been left in Amurru to secure the port of Sumur, and it was to play a critical part in the battle. Sherden troops also fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and this is the first record of them as Egyptian mercenaries; they later appear among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> who raided the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>.</p><p>The battle is believed to be the largest ever fought involving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, together 5,000 to 6,000. Mark Healy, in Armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaohs</a>, says the Egyptian chariot force cannot be stated precisely but could not have numbered fewer than 2,000 vehicles across the Amun, Re, Ptah and Set corps, on the assumption of about 500 in each, and that the Ne'arin's chariots may have to be added if they were not native Egyptian troops.</p><p>The Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> had mustered several allies, among them Rimisharrinaa, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Ramesses recorded a list of 19 allies whom Muwatalli brought to Kadesh, which interests Hittitologists because it shows how far Hittite influence reached. The Hittite king also hired local Shasu tribes. Muwatalli placed his troops behind "Old Kadesh", out of sight of the approaching Egyptians.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii.jpg" alt="Ramesses II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramses_II_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pbuergler, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a>' inscriptions say his advance guard, about 11 kilometres from Kadesh, met two Shasu nomads who said that the Hittite king was in the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, 200 kilometres away and too afraid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> to come south. Egyptian texts call this a false report ordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. A scout then brought in two Hittite prisoners, who said that the whole Hittite army stood ready behind the old city of Kadesh. By then Ramesses was alone with his bodyguard and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> division, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was sent to hurry up the Ptah and Set divisions.</p><p>While he was meeting his princes, Hittite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> crossed the river and struck the middle of the Re division as it marched towards him. Caught in the open, it scattered, some men fleeing north to the Amun camp with Hittite chariots behind them. The chariots then rounded north and attacked the camp, causing panic, but obstacles in the large camp slowed them. In the Egyptian account Ramesses was deserted and surrounded, called on Amun, and led several charges with his guard and survivors of the Re division.</p><p>The Hittites, believing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> routed, stopped to plunder the camp and were driven back towards the Orontes, where Egyptian chariots overtook them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli</a>, who still held reserves and the town walls, sent another thousand chariots forward. The Ne'arin then arrived suddenly from Amurru, and the Ptah division came up from the south, threatening the Hittite rear. After six unsuccessful Hittite charges the survivors, pinned against the river, left their chariots and tried to swim it, and many drowned, as Egyptian accounts tell it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii.jpg" alt="Hattusili III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Felsrelief_von_Firaktin_Hattusili_III_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Krähenstein, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> were routed but kept Kadesh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a> could not support a long siege of the walled city, so he gathered his troops and went south towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. He proclaimed that he had routed his enemies and had not tried to take Kadesh. The Egyptian account says the two armies faced each other again the next day, with a stalemate as the result. Hittite records from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a> give a different ending, in which Ramesses was forced to leave Kadesh in defeat. Hittite records indicate that at least two of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli</a>'s brothers were killed; a third brother, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a>, probably fought there.</p><p>Muwatalli went on to campaign as far south as the Egyptian province of Upi, which he captured and placed under his brother Hattusili. Egypt's sphere of influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> was restricted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canaan">Canaan</a>, which was for a time threatened by revolts among Egypt's Levantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. In his eighth and ninth years Ramesses took the Hittite-ruled cities of Dapur and Tunip, but the strip of land between Amurru and Kadesh could not be held and returned to Hittite control within a year, and he had to march against Dapur again in his tenth year. Neither Egypt nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatti">Hatti</a> could decisively defeat the other in the region.</p><p>A treaty with the new Hittite king Hattusili III was signed some 15 years after Kadesh, in the 21st year of Ramesses II (1258 BCE), and ended the borderland conflicts.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="Abu Simbel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramsis,_Aswan_Governorate,_Egypt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">youssef_alam, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There is more evidence in texts and wall reliefs for this battle than for any other in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Ancient Near East</a>, but almost all of it is Egyptian. The main account is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a>' own, whose bombastic nature is recognised. It exists in two forms, the Poem and the Bulletin. The Poem has been questioned as true verse rather than a prose account like those of other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>, and the Bulletin is a caption to the reliefs. The Bulletin is repeated seven times and the Poem eight, in temples at Abydos, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesseum">Ramesseum</a>, and many small captions identify details of the scenes.</p><p>Further Egyptian evidence is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> Raifet and Papyrus Sallier III, and in a letter from Ramesses to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> answering Hattusili's complaint about the pharaoh's claims of victory. Hittite references, including that letter, have been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, but no annals describing the battle as part of a campaign have been found; it is mentioned in connection with other events, above all by Hattusili III, for whom it was a milestone in his career.</p><p>James Henry Breasted made the first scholarly reconstruction in 1903, from Egyptian sources. Historians disagree about who won. Trevor Bryce says both sides claimed victory: Ramesses had the upper hand at the end but failed to retake Amurru and Kadesh, the point of the dispute, so that he describes an Egyptian tactical victory on the field and a Hittite strategic one, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> kept the disputed territory. The date also depends on the chronology used, the high chronology placing it about 20 years earlier.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusa.jpg" alt="Hattusa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lion_Gate,_Hattusa_13_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Kadesh is the earliest pitched battle in recorded history for which details of tactics and formations are known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses</a> treated it as a personal triumph, the king who rallied his scattered troops after moving into an ambush, and he had the account carved on temple walls across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Hittite court remembered it differently, and the letter from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> shows that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s claims were contested between the two kings themselves.</p><p>Modern historians conclude that the battle ended in a draw in practical terms. It is held to be a turning point for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a>, who had developed new technologies and rearmed against years of Hittite incursions. Egyptian influence over Amurru and Kadesh seems to have been lost for good, and Egypt's gains in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in the following years did not last.</p><p>Its best-known result is the treaty. It was inscribed on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> tablet, of which a clay copy was found in the Hittite capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and is on display at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> Archaeology Museum; an Egyptian version survives on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>. A large replica hangs at the headquarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. Peace brought intermarriage between the two courts, and Hattusili III and Ramesses II developed close relations.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/song-of-the-sea">Song of the Sea<span>c. 1000 BCE</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Near Kadesh on the Orontes in 1274 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a> fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> battle that nearly broke the Egyptian army. Both kings claimed victory, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> kept Kadesh, and about 15 years later the two empires made a treaty.</em></p>

